#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.12"
# dependencies = ["textual==8.2.8"]
# ///
"""Show what Claude is working on and where Ben is needed, as a bead tree in the terminal.

The board runs in one tmux pane beside `claude agents`. Its tree opens on every open bead,
grouped by area and then by epic, with the busiest area unfolded. o, d and c pick the list of
open, deferred or closed beads. n, v, p and / each narrow that list, and the title shows every
step with the number of beads left, such as Open 127 › now 42 › P0 18. The detail pane below
the tree leads with what the epic's goal loses if the bead is skipped. The fields come from
bead_model.py, which also holds every bd and claude call the board makes.

  bd-tui                 open the board in this terminal
  bd-tui --window        inside tmux: open a window with claude agents on the left, bd-tui on the right
  bd-tui --export FILE   read a saved `bd export` instead of running bd
  bd-tui --refresh 120   reread the beads every 120 s instead of every 60 s; 0 rereads only on r

The board rereads the beads every minute, so a bead that another session closes or claims
moves without a key press. A reread that finds no change only updates the read time.

Keys never use Ctrl-h, Ctrl-j, Ctrl-k or Ctrl-l, because vim-tmux-navigator moves between
panes with them. m marks a closed result reviewed and m again undoes it. Only Ben presses m,
because the rules reserve the reviewed label for him.

Shift-S opens a menu that moves the selected bead to open, in progress, deferred or closed.
Closed asks for the result in one line and marks it reviewed, because Ben wrote it. Deferred
asks for an optional date and reason, and open and in progress act at once. Esc cancels the
menu and its prompt. When a bead leaves the list, the cursor moves to the bead after it.

Text is plain weight by default. Bold marks only a bead's title and the section headings, and
colour carries status, so a narrow pane still reads at a glance.
"""
